fm_doc::register_doc! {
    name: "acos",
    aliases: [],
    section: "mathfunctions",
    summary: "Inverse Trigonometric Arccosine Function",
    body: r#"
## Usage
Computes the `acos` function for its argument.  The general
syntax for its use is

```text
  y = acos(x)
```
where `x` is an `n`-dimensional array of numerical type.
Integer types are promoted to the `double` type prior to
calculation of the `acos` function.  Output `y` is of the
same size and type as the input `x`, (unless `x` is an
integer, in which case `y` is a `double` type).

## Function Internals
Mathematically, the `acos` function is defined for all
arguments `x` as

$$
 \mathrm{acos} x \equiv \frac{pi}{2} + i \log \left(i x +
  \sqrt{1-x^2}\right).
$$
For real valued variables `x` in the range `[-1,1]`, the function is
computed directly using the standard C library's numerical `acos`
function. For both real and complex arguments `x`, note that generally

$$
  \mathrm{acos}(\cos(x)) \neq x,
$$

## Example
The following code demonstates the `acos` function over the range
`[-1,1]`.

```fm-exec:figure
t = linspace(-1,1);
plot(t,acos(t))
```
"#,
}

fm_doc::register_doc! {
    name: "acosd",
    aliases: [],
    section: "mathfunctions",
    summary: "Inverse Cosine Degrees Function",
    body: r#"
## Usage
Computes the inverse cosine of the argument, but returns
the argument in degrees instead of radians (as is the case
for `acos`. The syntax for its use is

```text
   y = acosd(x)
```

## Examples
The inverse cosine of `sqrt(2)/2` should be 45 degrees:

```fm-exec
acosd(sqrt(2)/2)
```

and the inverse cosine of `0.5` should be 60 degrees:

```fm-exec
acosd(0.5)
```
"#,
}

fm_doc::register_doc! {
    name: "acosh",
    aliases: [],
    section: "mathfunctions",
    summary: "Inverse Hyperbolic Cosine Function",
    body: r#"
## Usage
Computes the inverse hyperbolic cosine of its argument.  The general
syntax for its use is

```text
  y = acosh(x)
```
where `x` is an `n`-dimensional array of numerical type.

## Function Internals
The `acosh` function is computed from the formula

$$
   \cosh^{-1}(x) = \log\left(x + (x^2 - 1)^0.5\right)
$$
where the `log` (and square root) is taken in its most general sense.

## Examples
Here is a simple plot of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function

```fm-exec:figure
x = linspace(1,pi);
plot(x,acosh(x)); grid('on');
```
"#,
}

